<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>A friable, exophytic cervical mass about 4 cm across in a 31-year-old woman who has never had cervical screening, with four months of bleeding after intercourse and between periods and iron deficiency anemia. A punch biopsy, HPV test and cytology were taken today. Please see her on the day the biopsy result is given, to confirm the diagnosis, stage the disease, advise on treatment by stage, and counsel her about fertility and ovarian function, and help us give her the result as she has asked. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within one to two weeks, on the day of her biopsy result. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the biopsy confirms cancer, what is its type, and what is its FIGO 2018 stage, and which imaging is needed to stage it?</item><item>What treatment do you recommend for that stage, and is she a candidate for surgery or for chemoradiation?</item><item>What are her options to preserve fertility or ovarian function before treatment, and how much time do they need?</item><item>How should her bleeding, pain and anemia be managed before and during treatment?</item><item>Will you help us give her the diagnosis directly, in Spanish, in the way she has asked, and plan how information is shared with her from here on?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Four months of bleeding after intercourse and between periods, with heavier periods, tiredness, breathlessness on stairs, and a dull pelvic ache. Never had cervical screening; no HPV vaccine; one male partner; no pregnancies. Never smoked. Speculum examination: a friable exophytic mass about 4 cm across on the cervix that bleeds on contact; bimanual examination limited by tenderness; no inguinal nodes. Urine pregnancy test negative. Hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, mean cell volume 72 fL, ferritin 6 ng/mL, white cells 7.1 and platelets 398 x10^3/uL: iron deficiency anemia from blood loss; ferrous sulfate started. Pelvic pain 4 of 10; acetaminophen, not anti-inflammatory painkillers. PHQ-2 1. Spanish is her preferred language for health care; please book a professional medical interpreter, not a family member. She asked that all results be given to her first, in person, and that she alone decide what her family and colleagues are told [S]</paragraph><list><item>She has bled after intercourse and between periods for months and is exhausted, and she wants to know the cause. [S]</item><item>Whatever the results show, she wants to be told the truth herself, in Spanish, before anyone else is told. [S]</item><item>She wants to keep working and to understand how any treatment would affect her body and her chance of having children. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>She was born Eva María Ibarguren on May 7, 1919, in Los Toldos, a village in the Province of Buenos Aires, Argentina; her marriage contract later gave her birth date as May 7, 1922. [D]</item><item>She married Juan Perón on October 22, 1945, and became Argentina&apos;s first lady after his election in 1946. Her work for the poor made her a powerful political figure, and she is best known as Evita. [D]</item><item>Her medical ordeal began in January 1950, when she was 30: she fainted and underwent an appendectomy. Despite persistent weakness and anemia from vaginal bleeding, she delayed further tests. [D]</item><item>The Pap smear, which can find cervical cancer at its earliest stage, was developed in 1942, but in 1950 it was not yet standard medical care in the United States or Argentina. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

