<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Bleeding after intercourse and between periods, with heavier periods, for about four months, with tiredness and a dull pelvic ache. Never had cervical screening. Speculum examination shows a friable, exophytic mass about 4 cm across on the cervix that bleeds on contact. Punch biopsy, HPV test and cytology taken; urgent gynecologic oncology consultation booked: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Iron deficiency anemia from chronic blood loss: hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, mean cell volume 72 fL, ferritin 6 ng/mL, with tiredness and breathlessness on stairs: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Diagnosis of a visible cervical lesion: punch biopsy at this visit, an HPV test and cytology, a pregnancy test, a blood count and ferritin; a lesion that looks like cancer is biopsied directly rather than managed by the screening algorithm. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Cervical Cancer (Version 1.2027); 2019 ASCCP Risk-Based Management Consensus Guidelines for Abnormal Cervical Cancer Screening Tests and Cancer Precursors (2019) [S]</item><item>Truthful disclosure as she asked: the biopsy result given to her in person, in Spanish, at a visit with time set aside, before anyone else is told; she chooses who is present and who is told; a professional medical interpreter for clinicians who do not speak Spanish, never a family member; information shared step by step, checking what she wants to know. Guideline: Patient-Clinician Communication: American Society of Clinical Oncology Consensus Guideline (2017) [S]</item><item>Iron deficiency anemia: ferrous sulfate daily, a blood count at about one month, a ferritin at the end of the course, and acetaminophen rather than anti-inflammatory painkillers for pelvic pain. Guideline: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia monograph (2026) [S]</item><item ID="referral-order">Referral to gynecologic oncology, Henrietta Osei-Bonsu, MD, ordered 2026-02-09 [S]</item></list></text>

